WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.902
-It is an honor to introduce
my first guest.

00:00:02.902 --> 00:00:05.705
He's the 44th President
of the United States

00:00:05.705 --> 00:00:07.473 line:0%
and the author
of this best-selling

00:00:07.473 --> 00:00:09.742 line:0%
new book called
"A Promised Land."

00:00:09.742 --> 00:00:13.179
Here is President Barack Obama.

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:15.181
Hi, President --
-Jimmy.

00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:18.251
-Thank you so much for
coming back to the show.

00:00:18.251 --> 00:00:20.820
You look great.
And I know we don't really

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:22.455
have a way of really
keeping in touch.

00:00:22.455 --> 00:00:25.124
But, gosh,
I really missed you, bud.

00:00:25.124 --> 00:00:26.359
-I missed you.

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:31.564
Although, I kind of like
the whole disheveled --

00:00:31.564 --> 00:00:33.833
-[ Laughs ]
-You know, sweater.

00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:35.334
-No. No, no.
-Slightly --

00:00:35.334 --> 00:00:36.836
-I can't --
-Little bit of stubble look.

00:00:36.836 --> 00:00:38.037
-No.
-Now, you're back

00:00:38.037 --> 00:00:40.406
behind a desk, tie.
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:40.406 --> 00:00:42.908
-I can't -- for you --
-You know, hair product.

00:00:42.908 --> 00:00:46.012
-Yeah. I mean, I'm swearing swim
shorts, swim trunks, under here,

00:00:46.012 --> 00:00:47.480
but I'm not going
to show you that.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.281
But, yeah, I got to dress
up for you.

00:00:49.281 --> 00:00:52.585
I always think of the time
when I came to the White House

00:00:52.585 --> 00:00:54.253
for the Fourth of July
and I opened up

00:00:54.253 --> 00:00:56.422
for the Foo Fighters
for a veterans...

00:00:56.422 --> 00:00:57.824
-Yeah.
-...party that you had,

00:00:57.824 --> 00:00:59.292
and I got -- I was so nervous.

00:00:59.292 --> 00:01:01.160
I got really dressed up
there as well.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:04.129
And I was wearing -- I think I
was wearing a three-piece suit,

00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:06.666
and it was probably 98 degrees.

00:01:06.666 --> 00:01:08.801
-It was really hot.
-It was ridiculous --

00:01:08.801 --> 00:01:11.037
And Michelle was like,
"Oh, you poor thing.

00:01:11.037 --> 00:01:12.471
Loosen your tie or something."

00:01:12.471 --> 00:01:14.306
I mean, there was sweat
coming through my collar,

00:01:14.306 --> 00:01:16.342
and you were like,
"Didn't you get the memo?

00:01:16.342 --> 00:01:18.211
It's a barbecue."
And I go --

00:01:18.211 --> 00:01:20.046
-Come on.
-I was awful.

00:01:20.046 --> 00:01:23.148
I was sweating so much.
But, yeah.

00:01:23.148 --> 00:01:24.651
Those were so many --

00:01:24.651 --> 00:01:29.055
so many fun memories
hanging out with you, and --

00:01:29.055 --> 00:01:31.357 line:0%
Congrats on the book.
Here it is.

00:01:31.357 --> 00:01:32.758 line:0%
Great cover, by the way.

00:01:32.758 --> 00:01:34.360 line:0%
Great look.
-Thank you.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.329 line:0%
-Just crisp, clean.

00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:40.366
It's a -- it's not a quick read.

00:01:40.366 --> 00:01:41.901
But, it is a --
you get your money's worth.

00:01:41.901 --> 00:01:44.370
Look at this.
I mean, that's a big book.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:45.571
-You know what?

00:01:45.571 --> 00:01:47.407
I think you can
handle it, Jimmy.

00:01:47.407 --> 00:01:48.775
I really do.

00:01:48.775 --> 00:01:51.244
-Well, I mean, I got up
to the part with the pictures.

00:01:51.244 --> 00:01:53.212
And that's where I got
really excited.

00:01:53.212 --> 00:01:54.747
[ Laughter ]

00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:57.216
I -- before we get into
everything, how is the family?

00:01:57.216 --> 00:01:59.452
How is Michelle?
How are the girls?

00:01:59.452 --> 00:02:00.620
-You know, Michelle is great.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:02.388
She sends her love.
-Aww.

00:02:02.388 --> 00:02:05.357
-The girls have been with us
throughout the,

00:02:05.357 --> 00:02:07.026
you know, pandemic.

00:02:07.026 --> 00:02:10.096
Yeah, they've been doing
remote college.

00:02:10.096 --> 00:02:13.233
Malia's a senior at Harvard.

00:02:13.233 --> 00:02:16.302
And Sasha's a sophomore
at Michigan.

00:02:16.302 --> 00:02:18.704
-Wow. That's great.
-So...

00:02:18.704 --> 00:02:22.708
you know, as we've talked
about parenting,

00:02:22.708 --> 00:02:25.377
now I'm a little bit ahead
of you in age.

00:02:25.377 --> 00:02:27.480
-How is it?
-You go through this cycle.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.915
They love you.
They think you're terrific.

00:02:29.915 --> 00:02:31.584
You get to around 12.
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:31.584 --> 00:02:32.986
-They think you're a loser.

00:02:32.986 --> 00:02:35.521
-Yeah.
I got my first eye roll, yeah.

00:02:35.521 --> 00:02:38.724
-But by the time they come
back to you and, in this case,

00:02:38.724 --> 00:02:42.028
you know,
Sasha's now 19, Malia's 22.

00:02:42.028 --> 00:02:44.363
You know, they, suddenly,
like you again.

00:02:44.363 --> 00:02:46.532
-[ Laughs ]
-And they're interesting

00:02:46.532 --> 00:02:48.434
and they're smart,
and they're funnier

00:02:48.434 --> 00:02:50.402
than you are.
-Yeah.

00:02:50.402 --> 00:02:53.339
-So it's been a joy
to have them around.

00:02:53.339 --> 00:02:55.842
You know, I think they have,
obviously,

00:02:55.842 --> 00:02:57.676
started getting cabin fever

00:02:57.676 --> 00:02:59.512
hanging out with us
as much as they are.

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:01.180
But I don't mind it.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:04.384
-I got my first eye roll
the other day,

00:03:04.384 --> 00:03:07.053
which was pretty interesting.
-Oh, man.

00:03:07.053 --> 00:03:08.888
How'd do you handle it?

00:03:08.888 --> 00:03:11.757
-I couldn't -- I really --
It really broke my heart.

00:03:11.757 --> 00:03:13.126
I go,
"You're my number one fan.

00:03:13.126 --> 00:03:15.661
You can't --
you laugh at everything.

00:03:15.661 --> 00:03:18.498
Every joke I tell,
you laugh at.

00:03:18.498 --> 00:03:20.833
I -- I -- And she was like...
-Oh, man.

00:03:20.833 --> 00:03:23.903
-Yeah.
-Wait till they're 14. Whoo!

00:03:23.903 --> 00:03:26.405
-I cant -- I can't even.
I can't even.

00:03:26.405 --> 00:03:28.607
Are you -- in this
past four years,

00:03:28.607 --> 00:03:31.043
I think the last time
I talked to you,

00:03:31.043 --> 00:03:33.646
we were talking about what you
were most excited about doing,

00:03:33.646 --> 00:03:35.414
and I think you were like,

00:03:35.414 --> 00:03:37.483
just even pressing
a button on an elevator,

00:03:37.483 --> 00:03:39.885
or, it was like the simplest
little things

00:03:39.885 --> 00:03:41.620
that you did not do.

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:44.657
Have you done that?
Have you taken out the trash?

00:03:44.657 --> 00:03:46.859
Have you --
-I -- yeah, look,

00:03:46.859 --> 00:03:50.930
I mean, there's a bunch of
honey do list items

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:53.399
that I have been carrying out.

00:03:53.399 --> 00:03:56.469
Don't seem quite as romantic
as they did at the time.

00:03:56.469 --> 00:03:58.070
-Yeah.

00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:01.407
-And, you know, I've driven
a couple of times,

00:04:01.407 --> 00:04:03.742
although in very restricted
areas.

00:04:03.742 --> 00:04:06.345
-Stick shift or automatic?
-And much to the --

00:04:06.345 --> 00:04:09.248
Uh, automatic.
-Me too.

00:04:09.248 --> 00:04:12.918
-You know, I didn't want
to cause, you know, havoc.

00:04:12.918 --> 00:04:15.254
-[ Laughs ] Yeah.

00:04:15.254 --> 00:04:18.725
-But, you know, the thing
that I haven't been able to do

00:04:18.725 --> 00:04:20.626
that I thought I was
going to be able to do --

00:04:20.626 --> 00:04:24.063
and Michelle knew I was
an idiot to think this way,

00:04:24.063 --> 00:04:26.332
was, I thought I could,
like, go around

00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:28.234
and take a walk sometimes.

00:04:28.234 --> 00:04:31.704
And it turns out that...
-Yeah, you're you.

00:04:31.704 --> 00:04:34.673
-...I can't do that.
I cannot -- I can't do that.

00:04:34.673 --> 00:04:37.510
So -- Although we've been
doing some bike rides.

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:39.712
'Cause you're moving fast enough
that even if they...

00:04:39.712 --> 00:04:42.247
-They go, "Hey, is that --"
-...spot you,

00:04:42.247 --> 00:04:43.682
it's too late.
-Yeah, exactly.

00:04:43.682 --> 00:04:45.117
-You're gone by that time.

00:04:45.117 --> 00:04:46.552
-Probably it's even bet--
if you --

00:04:46.552 --> 00:04:48.287
You can't even do it alone,
I'm just saying,

00:04:48.287 --> 00:04:49.956
but if it's you and Michelle
and the kids,

00:04:49.956 --> 00:04:52.024
everyone's like,
"Wha-- wha-- what, what?

00:04:52.024 --> 00:04:54.894
That's like --"
People would just freak out.

00:04:54.894 --> 00:04:56.561
-Well, the thing about
Michelle when we go

00:04:56.561 --> 00:04:59.031
bike riding together,

00:04:59.031 --> 00:05:03.403
she's this -- she thinks
she's in a race, right?

00:05:03.403 --> 00:05:06.605
I mean, it --
on the Alps or something.

00:05:06.605 --> 00:05:08.307
And she's just powering
through,

00:05:08.307 --> 00:05:10.109
and she's about a mile
ahead of me, and...

00:05:10.109 --> 00:05:11.477
-Oh, no.
-I'm sort of meandering,

00:05:11.477 --> 00:05:13.045
trying to figure out
how to shift gears, and so --

00:05:13.045 --> 00:05:14.713
-Yeah, I'm cruising, yeah.
Me too.

00:05:14.713 --> 00:05:18.884
-Yeah, it's not that romantic.
-It's more of a workout.

00:05:18.884 --> 00:05:21.620
-It's more of a workout.

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:22.988
-For this, you took a deep dive

00:05:22.988 --> 00:05:25.524
into your journey
to the White House,

00:05:25.524 --> 00:05:28.628
and your first few years
as president.

00:05:28.628 --> 00:05:30.830
You also released
an audiobook version,

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:33.299
which you narrate,
which I think is great.

00:05:33.299 --> 00:05:36.669
But I was going to ask you,
when it came time to record it,

00:05:36.669 --> 00:05:38.571
was there any part of you
that was kicking yourself

00:05:38.571 --> 00:05:40.906
for writing such a long book?

00:05:40.906 --> 00:05:43.809
[ Laughter ]
-Absolutely.

00:05:43.809 --> 00:05:46.345
You know, you don't --

00:05:46.345 --> 00:05:49.715
You don't realize
how exhausting it is

00:05:49.715 --> 00:05:52.185
to just sit there and read.

00:05:52.185 --> 00:05:55.021
And it gives you
a newfound respect for,

00:05:55.021 --> 00:05:58.323
you know, professional actors
and narrators and so forth,

00:05:58.323 --> 00:06:01.460
because after about three hours
I was whooped.

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:03.262
-Yeah, exactly.
-And, you know, you look,

00:06:03.262 --> 00:06:04.931
"Really? We only got through
ten pages?

00:06:04.931 --> 00:06:06.232
What do you mean?"

00:06:06.232 --> 00:06:08.367
-You're like, "Hey,
can we get Tom Cruise

00:06:08.367 --> 00:06:10.136
to do this or something?"

00:06:10.136 --> 00:06:14.506
-Exactly.
But, you know,

00:06:14.506 --> 00:06:16.408
the goal of my --

00:06:16.408 --> 00:06:20.179
my book was to

00:06:20.179 --> 00:06:24.717
tell a story
about, yes, policy,

00:06:24.717 --> 00:06:27.119
and you know,
the Affordable Care Act

00:06:27.119 --> 00:06:30.690
and how we got Bin Laden
and all that stuff.

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:33.826
But also to try to pull
the curtain back

00:06:33.826 --> 00:06:38.297
and give people a sense
of what it's like for a family

00:06:38.297 --> 00:06:40.665
to go on this weird journey.

00:06:40.665 --> 00:06:43.869
And -- and, you know,

00:06:43.869 --> 00:06:48.273
how Michelle and the girls
and I

00:06:48.273 --> 00:06:52.478
had to try to hang on to

00:06:52.478 --> 00:06:54.880
our sense of who we were
and normalcy,

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:58.351
and with highs and lows

00:06:58.351 --> 00:07:01.354
and tensions and stresses.
-Yeah.

00:07:01.354 --> 00:07:03.523
-And kind of come out
of the other end intact.

00:07:03.523 --> 00:07:06.559
And so part of my goal here

00:07:06.559 --> 00:07:08.928
was not just to give
some dry report,

00:07:08.928 --> 00:07:11.230
but, you know,
to describe for people

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:12.632
what it's like,
you know, the first night

00:07:12.632 --> 00:07:15.501
you sleep in the White House
and you kind of realize,

00:07:15.501 --> 00:07:17.603
"What the heck?
How'd I end up here?" Or...

00:07:17.603 --> 00:07:19.505
-Yeah. Do you remember
the first thing you saw

00:07:19.505 --> 00:07:22.174
when you actually walked
into the White House?

00:07:22.174 --> 00:07:24.343
-Were you like -- Did you
look at like a phone

00:07:24.343 --> 00:07:26.345
or an old painting or something
and you're like,

00:07:26.345 --> 00:07:29.081
"Wait, I'm actually --
I'm the White House"?

00:07:29.081 --> 00:07:30.983
-All of the above.
-Yeah.

00:07:30.983 --> 00:07:32.451
-And, you know,
the interesting thing is,

00:07:32.451 --> 00:07:34.653
is that you don't --

00:07:34.653 --> 00:07:38.557
you literally are moved in
while you're being sworn in.

00:07:38.557 --> 00:07:39.925
So you haven't --

00:07:39.925 --> 00:07:41.260
The Bushes were very gracious.

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:43.495
They had given us a tour,
but, you know,

00:07:43.495 --> 00:07:46.065
you're not really
paying attention.

00:07:46.065 --> 00:07:48.534
It kind of feels like
you're in a museum.

00:07:48.534 --> 00:07:50.269
Uh...

00:07:50.269 --> 00:07:54.706
You get inaugurated.
There are a bunch of balls.

00:07:54.706 --> 00:07:57.008
You're shaking hands
and schmoozing

00:07:57.008 --> 00:07:59.178
and watching parades.

00:07:59.178 --> 00:08:02.348
And then you go home,
quote/unquote,

00:08:02.348 --> 00:08:05.518
and suddenly
you're in this place where

00:08:05.518 --> 00:08:08.420
the Gettysburg Address
is next to --

00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:12.091
in the next room, and...

00:08:12.091 --> 00:08:14.894
you know, you realize
that your life

00:08:14.894 --> 00:08:17.263
is not going to be the same,

00:08:17.263 --> 00:08:20.800
and trying to make sure that
you're maintaining perspective.

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:24.102
You know, I discuss a lot
in the book...

00:08:24.102 --> 00:08:26.639
you know, one of the greatest
accomplishments, I think,

00:08:26.639 --> 00:08:29.575
of my presidency
for both Michelle and me

00:08:29.575 --> 00:08:33.913
was raising two girls
in this very strange environment

00:08:33.913 --> 00:08:36.749
and them turning out to be
these wonderful,

00:08:36.749 --> 00:08:38.984
not at all entitled,
you know,

00:08:38.984 --> 00:08:41.988
kind, thoughtful people.
-Yeah.

00:08:41.988 --> 00:08:43.588
-Partly because
of my mother-in-law,

00:08:43.588 --> 00:08:45.791
who, you know, she --

00:08:45.791 --> 00:08:49.395
we were very lucky to have her
come stay with us.

00:08:49.395 --> 00:08:50.997
And, you know,
she'd look at the girls

00:08:50.997 --> 00:08:52.498
if they were acting up
and she'd say,

00:08:52.498 --> 00:08:53.900
"What are you guys doing?"
-Yeah.

00:08:53.900 --> 00:08:55.735
-"You know you didn't
do anything."

00:08:55.735 --> 00:08:56.836
-Good.

00:08:56.836 --> 00:08:58.070
-"You're just here
for the ride."

00:08:58.070 --> 00:08:59.438
-You need that, yeah.
-"Settle down."

00:08:59.438 --> 00:09:01.540
-Yeah, and just grounding them.

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:04.409
So...

00:09:04.409 --> 00:09:06.946
And part of the reason
I wanted to personalize it was

00:09:06.946 --> 00:09:09.382
so that young people
who read it,

00:09:09.382 --> 00:09:10.849
who are interested
in public service,

00:09:10.849 --> 00:09:15.788
who are interested in government
or just changing the world,

00:09:15.788 --> 00:09:17.222
they can kind of see, "Okay,

00:09:17.222 --> 00:09:20.759
here's somebody
who is kind of normal

00:09:20.759 --> 00:09:25.263
and was able to do
some important stuff.

00:09:25.263 --> 00:09:26.865
And maybe, I can, too."
-Yeah.

00:09:26.865 --> 00:09:28.867
-And hopefully inspires them
to get involved.

00:09:28.867 --> 00:09:30.268
-Yeah.
You wrote that,

00:09:30.268 --> 00:09:32.672
yeah, you want this --
this book is for, like,

00:09:32.672 --> 00:09:34.807
young people, in their 20s,

00:09:34.807 --> 00:09:36.575
and who want
to change the world.

00:09:36.575 --> 00:09:41.681
I was going to ask you, are you
inspired by your own daughters?

00:09:41.681 --> 00:09:42.915
-I am.

00:09:42.915 --> 00:09:45.050
You know, they and their
friends, you know,

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:50.689
during this summer after
the tragedy of George Floyd

00:09:50.689 --> 00:09:54.126
and the protests and activism,

00:09:54.126 --> 00:09:56.161
you know, they found ways
to get involved

00:09:56.161 --> 00:09:59.599
that were very smart
and thoughtful and practical.

00:09:59.599 --> 00:10:02.134
And they got
their friends involved.

00:10:02.134 --> 00:10:06.772
This whole generation of young
people coming up

00:10:06.772 --> 00:10:09.775
are smart,
thoughtful, sophisticated.

00:10:09.775 --> 00:10:14.647
And they really believe
all the platitudes

00:10:14.647 --> 00:10:16.448
about everybody's equal

00:10:16.448 --> 00:10:18.317
and we should treat
everybody fairly.

00:10:18.317 --> 00:10:21.687
And, you know,
we don't like racism

00:10:21.687 --> 00:10:24.089
and we don't like discriminating
against people

00:10:24.089 --> 00:10:27.226
because of their
sexual orientation.

00:10:27.226 --> 00:10:28.894
It's second nature to them.

00:10:28.894 --> 00:10:37.135
And there is a courage and
conviction that they bring to it

00:10:37.135 --> 00:10:40.306
that really is inspiring
and makes me optimistic.

00:10:40.306 --> 00:10:45.644
You know, it's a matter of us
old heads getting out of the way

00:10:45.644 --> 00:10:47.679
and making sure
we don't break things so badly

00:10:47.679 --> 00:10:49.715
that by the time
they're in charge,

00:10:49.715 --> 00:10:51.517
you know, it's not too late.

00:10:51.517 --> 00:10:52.985
-Yeah.

00:10:52.985 --> 00:10:55.887
There's also some good stories
in here about you choosing

00:10:55.887 --> 00:10:58.190
Joe Biden as your
vice president,

00:10:58.190 --> 00:11:02.795
and you do a great job in there
describing his character.

00:11:02.795 --> 00:11:04.196
I've met him a couple times.

00:11:04.196 --> 00:11:08.033
He's just very memorable,
very charming.

00:11:08.033 --> 00:11:10.669
What is your favorite memory
of working

00:11:10.669 --> 00:11:13.572
or spending time with Joe Biden?

00:11:13.572 --> 00:11:19.044
-You know, the thing about Joe
is he genuinely loves people.

00:11:19.044 --> 00:11:20.913
Everybody.
-Yeah.

00:11:20.913 --> 00:11:23.615
-It's not a put-on.
You know, there's some folks,

00:11:23.615 --> 00:11:26.218
and I'm sure you've seen them
in the entertainment industry,

00:11:26.218 --> 00:11:29.487
do not name names, where they're
all fun and laughs,

00:11:29.487 --> 00:11:31.957
you know, in public and then...
-Cameras off.

00:11:31.957 --> 00:11:35.427
-[ Laughs ] Cameras off
and they're curmudgeons.

00:11:35.427 --> 00:11:37.029
-Oh, yeah.
-Right?

00:11:37.029 --> 00:11:39.798
-Joe is exactly
who he appears to be.

00:11:39.798 --> 00:11:44.770
And, you know, probably
my strongest memories of Joe

00:11:44.770 --> 00:11:49.308
are when we were
campaigning together.

00:11:49.308 --> 00:11:52.211
You know, I'd work the rope
lines after I'd made a speech,

00:11:52.211 --> 00:11:54.813
you know, and I'd hug babies.

00:11:54.813 --> 00:11:57.016
-Yeah.
-You know, kiss grandmas.

00:11:57.016 --> 00:12:01.186
And we'd go around and
shake hands and all this stuff.

00:12:01.186 --> 00:12:04.857
And I felt like
I'd shaken every hand

00:12:04.857 --> 00:12:07.425
and I'm finished
with the rope line.

00:12:07.425 --> 00:12:11.864
And I look back and, like,
Joe's just starting.

00:12:11.864 --> 00:12:13.666
I mean, I think he's gotten

00:12:13.666 --> 00:12:16.235
the life story
of the first four people.

00:12:16.235 --> 00:12:19.672
-Yeah.
-And so, campaigning with Joe

00:12:19.672 --> 00:12:22.174
meant you were going to be late.

00:12:22.174 --> 00:12:27.512
But because he really cares
about, you know, hearing people

00:12:27.512 --> 00:12:29.381
and letting them know
that they're important.

00:12:29.381 --> 00:12:32.717
And I think that fundamental
character of his

00:12:32.717 --> 00:12:34.620
is exactly what we need
right now

00:12:34.620 --> 00:12:40.258
after so much division
and anger and frustration.

00:12:40.258 --> 00:12:42.994
Just having somebody
who likes people,

00:12:42.994 --> 00:12:46.198
cares about 'em,
knows their struggles.

00:12:46.198 --> 00:12:47.599
I think that's going to make
a big difference,

00:12:47.599 --> 00:12:51.136
and I think Kamala will bring
some of those same values

00:12:51.136 --> 00:12:53.538
to bear when they get sworn in.

00:12:53.538 --> 00:12:56.642
-Even seeing him with,
unfortunately, with his boot,

00:12:56.642 --> 00:13:00.479
the -- he fractured
his foot or whatever,

00:13:00.479 --> 00:13:02.180
it just makes you --

00:13:02.180 --> 00:13:05.284
I'm actually going like,
man, this is exactly --

00:13:05.284 --> 00:13:07.185
Already, I'm more interested
in that --

00:13:07.185 --> 00:13:09.755
If that's a scandal,
I will take that.

00:13:09.755 --> 00:13:12.090
I just think, that's normal,
that's human,

00:13:12.090 --> 00:13:13.758
that's, you know...

00:13:13.758 --> 00:13:16.562
-That's what happens
with normal people.

00:13:16.562 --> 00:13:17.696
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:13:17.696 --> 00:13:20.132
-Playing with their dog,
so...

00:13:20.132 --> 00:13:22.501
-Was it tough for the past
four years,

00:13:22.501 --> 00:13:26.271
was it difficult to sit back
and not say anything?

00:13:26.271 --> 00:13:30.208
And go like, "I have to respect
the presidency, I understand."

00:13:30.208 --> 00:13:32.444
But -- 'cause I know,
I'm speaking

00:13:32.444 --> 00:13:35.013
for a lot of people,
where I'm just like, let's --

00:13:35.013 --> 00:13:38.783
let's call Obama right now
and get him to go, "Hey, enough.

00:13:38.783 --> 00:13:40.485
Hey, yo.

00:13:40.485 --> 00:13:42.086
Stop it.
You stop it."

00:13:42.086 --> 00:13:44.323
And just yell at him.

00:13:44.323 --> 00:13:46.625
-I -- Look, yeah.

00:13:46.625 --> 00:13:48.860
It was difficult as a citizen.

00:13:48.860 --> 00:13:50.962
I mean, people have asked me,
they asked me,

00:13:50.962 --> 00:13:54.499
even before I left office,

00:13:54.499 --> 00:13:57.035
"Are you going to miss
the presidency?"

00:13:57.035 --> 00:13:59.138
I like the fact that, you know,

00:13:59.138 --> 00:14:00.739
you have eight years
if you're lucky.

00:14:00.739 --> 00:14:04.576
And then it's somebody else's
turn and you pass the baton.

00:14:04.576 --> 00:14:07.312
And you become a citizen again.

00:14:07.312 --> 00:14:09.181
And because I think

00:14:09.181 --> 00:14:12.751
that constantly refreshes
our democracy.

00:14:12.751 --> 00:14:17.322
So, I wasn't one of these people
who had to be dragged off

00:14:17.322 --> 00:14:18.990
the stage, in that sense.

00:14:18.990 --> 00:14:24.263
-Yep.
-But there's no doubt that,

00:14:24.263 --> 00:14:27.265
let's talk about the pandemic,
for example, right now.

00:14:27.265 --> 00:14:31.002
We had written up
a pandemic playbook.

00:14:31.002 --> 00:14:33.939
We had -- I had experienced
a pandemic,

00:14:33.939 --> 00:14:37.109
as I write about in the book,
with H1N1,

00:14:37.109 --> 00:14:41.279
six months into my presidency
we were dealing with a pandemic.

00:14:41.279 --> 00:14:43.482
Then, we had Ebola.

00:14:43.482 --> 00:14:45.350
We had set up a whole structure.

00:14:45.350 --> 00:14:47.853
We had had a task force
in the White House.

00:14:47.853 --> 00:14:51.723
We had, you know,
deployed scientists

00:14:51.723 --> 00:14:55.160
as an early-warning system
in countries around the world

00:14:55.160 --> 00:14:56.728
to ensure their cooperation

00:14:56.728 --> 00:15:00.431
so that we get ahead of the game
if something like this happened.

00:15:00.431 --> 00:15:06.204
And so, when you see all that
discarded and ignored,

00:15:06.204 --> 00:15:09.742
just at the time
when you need it most,

00:15:09.742 --> 00:15:13.545
and knowing the consequences
for, you know,

00:15:13.545 --> 00:15:17.516
families across the country,
yes, that was frustrating.

00:15:17.516 --> 00:15:23.121
And you know, people I think
were surprised

00:15:23.121 --> 00:15:26.224
by my bluntness maybe,
during the campaign season.

00:15:26.224 --> 00:15:29.027
And they said, "Oh, he must've
had all this stuff bottled up."

00:15:29.027 --> 00:15:33.365
It wasn't so much that there was
a bunch of personal outrage

00:15:33.365 --> 00:15:36.135
bottled up,
as it was a genuine sense

00:15:36.135 --> 00:15:38.570
that, you know,
good government matters.

00:15:38.570 --> 00:15:44.176
And some of the norms
and, you know,

00:15:44.176 --> 00:15:46.145
practices we use
to just make sure

00:15:46.145 --> 00:15:50.215
that our democracy
is civil and functions,

00:15:50.215 --> 00:15:51.884
the kind of transition
that I write about

00:15:51.884 --> 00:15:55.854
that George Bush
and Laura Bush extended to us,

00:15:55.854 --> 00:15:58.357
and that we extended
to the Trumps,

00:15:58.357 --> 00:16:01.060
those kinds of things
are important.

00:16:01.060 --> 00:16:04.129
You know, sometimes, we think
of politics as so different

00:16:04.129 --> 00:16:07.066
from daily life, but it's not.

00:16:07.066 --> 00:16:10.735
You know, we don't have
rules about

00:16:10.735 --> 00:16:13.805
just being courteous
or being thoughtful

00:16:13.805 --> 00:16:17.676
or trying to hear
another person's point of view.

00:16:17.676 --> 00:16:19.912
We can't police that
all the time.

00:16:19.912 --> 00:16:23.148
It matters how we express
our values

00:16:23.148 --> 00:16:24.649
and interact
with each other every day.

00:16:24.649 --> 00:16:26.385
That's what
we teach our kids.

00:16:26.385 --> 00:16:28.487
-Yeah.
-And that matters

00:16:28.487 --> 00:16:31.923
in the White House
and in Congress as well.

00:16:31.923 --> 00:16:35.326
It's obviously been
a tough time for our country,

00:16:35.326 --> 00:16:38.196
but you are an optimist,
as am I.

00:16:38.196 --> 00:16:40.799
And you're
the "Yes, we can" guy,

00:16:40.799 --> 00:16:42.600
you're the "Audacity of Hope"
guy.

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:46.204
What keeps you feeling
optimistic?

00:16:46.204 --> 00:16:48.806
-I talked about it.
The young people that I meet.

00:16:48.806 --> 00:16:52.811
And, you know, Michelle and I
are focusing on

00:16:52.811 --> 00:16:55.814
building up our foundation,

00:16:55.814 --> 00:16:58.716
and our entire mission
is actually

00:16:58.716 --> 00:17:01.720
to train the next generation
of leaders.

00:17:01.720 --> 00:17:03.321
And, you know,
the Presidential Center

00:17:03.321 --> 00:17:06.492
we're building in Chicago
will be focused on

00:17:06.492 --> 00:17:10.495
creating training centers
and classrooms

00:17:10.495 --> 00:17:13.632
and convenings of these
amazing young people

00:17:13.632 --> 00:17:15.500
that you meet
all around the country

00:17:15.500 --> 00:17:17.202
who are working on
climate change

00:17:17.202 --> 00:17:19.637
and criminal justice reform

00:17:19.637 --> 00:17:21.940
and, you know,
educating kids.

00:17:21.940 --> 00:17:24.276
And when you talk to
these young people,

00:17:24.276 --> 00:17:27.879
not just in America
but around the world.

00:17:27.879 --> 00:17:30.215
It makes you optimistic.

00:17:30.215 --> 00:17:34.152
It gives you a sense that
they're as idealistic

00:17:34.152 --> 00:17:35.988
but maybe a little more
sophisticated

00:17:35.988 --> 00:17:39.324
and practical than any
previous generation.

00:17:39.324 --> 00:17:41.259
-There's so much more I want
to talk to you about.

00:17:41.259 --> 00:17:43.562
More with President Barack Obama
when we return, everybody.

